<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?> 

<section xml:id="section_1_3" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Preliminary: Exploring random processes</title>



<subsection> 
 <title>Random processes</title>
<p>  A <term>random process</term> is a repeatable process where the result for an 
  individual attempt is not known in advance but there is a predictable pattern to 
  the results in the long run. Playing <em>Let's Make a Deal</em> is an example of 
  a random process: The result for the next round of the game is uncertain, but if 
  the game is played many, many times, a predictable pattern is expected.  </p>
</subsection>

<subsection> 
 <title>Long-run proportion</title>
  <p>A <term>simulation</term> is an artificial re-creation of a random process.</p>

</subsection>

<subsection> 
  <title>Interpretation of probability</title>
  <p>
    The <term>probability</term> of an outcome is the long-run proportion of times an 
    outcome from a random process occurs if the process is repeated indefinitely under 
    identical conditions. A probability can be written as a decimal (0.3333), a fraction 
    (1/3), or a percentage (33.33%).
  </p>
<exercise>
 <title> Switch or stay</title>
<statement>
<p>How do you think the probability of winning with the switch strategy will compare to the probability of winning with the stay strategy? Briefly explain your reasoning.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<remark>
  <p>
    The sections in this chapter may have felt unrelated and brief, but they each 
    contributed important components of drawing conclusions from data, including 
    the six-step investigation process, variation in data, and exploring randomness 
    and probability.
  </p>
</remark>
</subsection>

</section>